{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789940825,"nonce":"VH70oQKrvNOjbdVMwTuBfcuBTKc=","title":"e2e_two_threads_run_wren_on_one_heap: threaded interpreter reads a freed ThreadedCode","message":"Under load, the e2e binary dies in execute_threaded at the first\nhandler load, `ldr x8, [ops + pc*32]`, with `ops` a garbage pointer\n(fault address 0x600000080, i.e. a small integer plus pc*32). The\n`\u0026ThreadedCode` the frame was entered with no longer points at live\nops: the other thread of the test replaced or dropped the closure's\nthreaded code while this one was running it.\n\nSeen on macOS (SIGSEGV, LLVM 21.1.8 build, load average 60, twice in\n~10 runs; 0 in the next 50) and on Windows CI at 3c5299c\n(STATUS_HEAP_CORRUPTION in the e2e binary; the two-threads tests were\nthe last running). Reproduced at 28c5853 too? Not in 8 runs, so no\nbisect yet; the pattern predates the frame-pointer walk.\n\nTwo views share one engine; ThreadedCode caches (per closure/function)\nare shared without a lock or an epoch. Needs either per-thread caches\nor a retire list that keeps a ThreadedCode alive while any frame may\nstill be executing it, the way retired_code keeps native bodies.","files":null}]}